window.PVPBOT_INDEX = [{"title": "Home", "page": "Getting started", "url": "index.html", "text": "AI-driven PvP bots for Paper and Spigot servers. Paper & Spigot · 1.21.11 and 26.2 PvpBot Bots that join your server as real players and fight the way people do: jump crits, shields, the mace, bows, pearls, cobwebs and water buckets. Built to train against and record with, not to win every fight. Download on Modrinth Quick start What's in the next update →"}, {"title": "What it is", "page": "Home", "url": "index.html#what-it-is", "text": "A PvpBot bot is a player with no client attached . To the server and every other plugin it is a person like anyone else: it has an inventory, armour, hunger, potion effects and a skin, and it takes knockback and damage the vanilla way. Nothing it does is frame-perfect. One skill dial, from 0 to 100, sets how quickly it reacts, how well it times a swing and how far off its aim lands. Even at 100 there is a gap to play around."}, {"title": "The manual", "page": "Home", "url": "index.html#the-manual", "text": "Getting started Installation Which jar to use, requirements, compatible plugins. Quick start A first bot, a first fight, a first faction. Next update Finished on GitHub, not on Modrinth yet. Reference Commands Every /pvpbot and /ghost command. Selectors Ordering one bot, a faction or a random group. Settings All 116 options, with defaults and limits. Features Combat Melee, mace, elytra mace, cart PvP, bows, shields. Movement Pathfinding, bridging, boats, guards and patrols. Factions War, peace, alliances, leaders and overfactions. Kits and presets Saved loadouts and difficulty profiles. Scripts Whole scenarios saved under a name. Companions Bots a player orders around in chat. Skins and names Real or random skins, themed name tables. Ghosts Scenery NPCs that replay a path or watch you. Help Troubleshooting Bots that are immortal, stuck or won't attack. Spigot edition What differs from th"}, {"title": "At a glance", "page": "Home", "url": "index.html#at-a-glance", "text": "Servers Paper 1.21.11 and 26.2, Spigot 1.21.11 and 26.2 (one jar each) Commands /pvpbot (alias /bot ) and /ghost Permission pvpbot.admin , ops by default Dependencies None. SkinsRestorer is used if it is installed. Download modrinth.com/plugin/pvp-bot-plugin-pvpbp"}, {"title": "Installation", "page": "Getting started", "url": "installation.html", "text": "Which jar to use, what the server needs and which plugins get along with PvpBot."}, {"title": "Requirements", "page": "Installation", "url": "installation.html#requirements", "text": "Server Version Java Jar Paper 26.2 25 or newer Paper edition Paper 1.21.11 21 or newer Paper edition (1.21.11 build) Spigot 26.2 25 or newer Spigot 26.2 edition Spigot 1.21.11 21 or newer Spigot 1.21.11 edition Each jar only works on its own platform and version: PvpBot uses server internals to create the bots, so a Paper jar won't start on Spigot and vice versa. The features are the same in every edition (see Spigot edition )."}, {"title": "Steps", "page": "Installation", "url": "installation.html#steps", "text": "Stop the server. Download the jar for your server and version from Modrinth . Put it in the plugins/ folder. Start the server. PvpBot creates plugins/PvpBot/config.yml with every setting commented. As an op, run /pvpbot spawn . If a bot appears, you're set. Updating from an older version You don't need to delete config.yml . New settings missing from the file use their default value; /pvpbot settings marks them with * until you change them with /pvpbot set ."}, {"title": "Permissions", "page": "Installation", "url": "installation.html#permissions", "text": "Permission Default Grants pvpbot.admin op Every /pvpbot (alias /bot ) and /ghost command. Regular players need no permission to command the bots you hand them: they do it by typing in chat (see Companions )."}, {"title": "Plugin files", "page": "Installation", "url": "installation.html#plugin-files", "text": "Everything lives in plugins/PvpBot/ : File Contents config.yml Settings, presets, skins, name tables, the companions' world. bots.yml The active bots, brought back after a restart if bots-relog is on. factions.yml Factions, members, relations, leaders, overfactions, per-faction settings and kit tables. kits.yml Saved kits. scripts.yml Saved scripts. paths.yml Patrol routes. ownership.yml Which player owns which bot. skin-cache.yml Skins already fetched, so restarts don't ask Mojang again. ghost-recordings/ Paths recorded with /ghost record . After editing a file by hand, run /pvpbot reload ."}, {"title": "Compatible plugins", "page": "Installation", "url": "installation.html#compatible-plugins", "text": ""}, {"title": "SkinsRestorer", "page": "Installation", "url": "installation.html#skinsrestorer", "text": "Optional. If it's installed, PvpBot stores each fetched skin directly in its storage, so SkinsRestorer doesn't overwrite it with a default one when the bot joins."}, {"title": "Login plugins (AuthMe, LoginTo)", "page": "Installation", "url": "installation.html#login-plugins-authme-loginto", "text": "A bot can't type /login , so login plugins would keep it locked forever (the usual symptom is a bot that hops on the spot and takes no damage). With auth-autologin: true , on by default, PvpBot registers every bot as already logged in. Nothing changes for real players. See Troubleshooting if that isn't enough."}, {"title": "Protection (WorldGuard, GriefPrevention, claims)", "page": "Installation", "url": "installation.html#protection-worldguard-griefprevention-claims", "text": "Every block a bot places or breaks goes through the server's normal events, so protection plugins see it and can refuse it. The same goes for the rails and carts of cart PvP."}, {"title": "Quick start", "page": "Getting started", "url": "quick-start.html", "text": "From your first bot to a small faction war, in a handful of commands. Every command below needs pvpbot.admin (ops have it). You can type /bot instead of /pvpbot ."}, {"title": "1. Your first bot", "page": "Quick start", "url": "quick-start.html#1-your-first-bot", "text": "/pvpbot spawn Marco A bot called Marco appears where you stand. If a Minecraft account has that name, the bot wears its skin; otherwise it gets a random one (see Skins and names ). Without a name, one is generated. A new bot leaves you alone for a few seconds ( spawn-grace ), then sizes you up for a moment ( reaction-ticks ) before attacking."}, {"title": "2. Give it some gear", "page": "Quick start", "url": "quick-start.html#2-give-it-some-gear", "text": "A new bot has an empty inventory and fights with its fists. The quickest fix is to save your own inventory as a kit and hand it over: /pvpbot kit save diamond /pvpbot kit give diamond Marco The bot puts on the best armour and holds the best weapon it carries by itself ( auto-equip )."}, {"title": "3. Make it fight", "page": "Quick start", "url": "quick-start.html#3-make-it-fight", "text": "/pvpbot attack Marco Steve /pvpbot spawn Luca /pvpbot fight Marco Luca /pvpbot stop Marco attack sends Marco after Steve. fight sets two bots on each other (even with bot-vs-bot off). stop calls it off."}, {"title": "4. Lots of bots at once", "page": "Quick start", "url": "quick-start.html#4-lots-of-bots-at-once", "text": "/pvpbot spawn 10 /pvpbot kit give diamond latest /pvpbot follow latest Steve circle This spawns 10 bots with generated names (50 at most per command), gives them all the kit and has them escort Steve in a circle. latest is a selector : it means everything the last spawn created, so you don't have to type ten names."}, {"title": "5. Two factions at war", "page": "Quick start", "url": "quick-start.html#5-two-factions-at-war", "text": "/pvpbot faction create red /pvpbot faction create blue /pvpbot spawn 5 /pvpbot faction join red latest /pvpbot spawn 5 /pvpbot faction join blue latest /pvpbot faction hostile red blue The two factions' bots now attack each other on sight. Real players are put in the player faction automatically. More on Factions ."}, {"title": "6. Change the difficulty", "page": "Quick start", "url": "quick-start.html#6-change-the-difficulty", "text": "/pvpbot preset casual /pvpbot preset sweaty /pvpbot preset off casual makes bots slow and sloppy, sweaty turns everything on with sharp reactions, and off goes back to config.yml as written. Or change a single setting: /pvpbot set skill 70 . The full list is on Settings ."}, {"title": "7. Clean up", "page": "Quick start", "url": "quick-start.html#7-clean-up", "text": "/pvpbot remove Marco /pvpbot removeall Next steps Save this sequence as a script and run it again with one command, or hand a bot to a player with /pvpbot owner set (see Companions )."}, {"title": "Next update", "page": "Getting started", "url": "next-update.html", "text": "Everything finished on GitHub but not in the Modrinth download yet. It all ships with the next release. Unreleased This page lists what is in PvpBot's source but not in the build on Modrinth. The rest of the wiki describes the Modrinth build. 98 new settings, a dozen new commands and a menu for nearly everything. Every existing setting keeps its meaning. One command leaves: /pvpbot tp rift (see Leaving PvpBot ). Most of the new behaviour is on by default, and the new techniques start off. In short: difficulty is no longer one slider, bots aim and land hits the way a player's crosshair does, an"}, {"title": "Difficulty knobs and presets", "page": "Next update", "url": "next-update.html#difficulty-knobs-and-presets", "text": "skill is now split into one knob per part of it. That way you can build a bot that aims perfectly but reacts slowly, or one that never misses a crit but can't clutch. A knob at -1 follows skill , so a config that only sets skill plays as it did. Six presets write every knob at once: easy , medium , hard , expert , professional and perfect . Every setting now resolves per bot first, then per faction, then globally. A bot's own values are kept by name in bot-settings.yml , so they survive respawns and restarts. /pvpbot difficulty [list] /pvpbot difficulty preset <easy|medium|hard|expert|professional|perfect> /pvpbot difficulty <knob> <value> /pvpbot difficulty bot <bot> [list|preset <level>|clear|<knob> <value>] /pvpbot difficulty faction <faction> [list|preset <level>|clear|<knob> <value>] Setting Default Values What it does aim -1 -1 – 100 How straight it aims - swings, pearls, charges, "}, {"title": "Playstyles", "page": "Next update", "url": "next-update.html#playstyles", "text": "A playstyle sets what a bot wants , on top of how well it plays: when it commits, heals, runs and chases. aggressive commits and stays in longer. safe heals and pearls out early. defensive stands its ground: it blocks more, punishes your mistakes, never chases past a few blocks (it stops, faces you and lets you come back), never backs off to reset, and heals where it stands. adaptive swings between aggressive and safe with the health race. balanced sits in the middle, and default plays as bots always have. Set it per bot (kept by name), per faction or globally. randomplaystyle rolls each bot its own, by weights you choose. /pvpbot playstyle list /pvpbot playstyle <style> <bots> /pvpbot playstyle clear <bots> /pvpbot playstyle faction <faction> <style|clear> /pvpbot randomplaystyle <bots> /pvpbot randomplaystylechances [list|reset|<style> <weight>] The opening is new too. A bot walks a ne"}, {"title": "The settings menu", "page": "Next update", "url": "next-update.html#the-settings-menu", "text": "/pvpbot settings opens a paged menu of every setting, generated from the same list the commands validate against, so nothing is ever missing from it. Left-click toggles or steps a value up, right-click steps it down, and shift steps by ten. Q takes a faction's or bot's own value off. Filter by group, search by word, and nudge the whole difficulty a preset up or down from the bottom row. It works on the Spigot edition too. /pvpbot settings the menu /pvpbot settings search <word> the menu, only what matches /pvpbot settings bot <bot> | faction <faction> one bot's or one faction's values /pvpbot settings list [group|word] printed in chat instead /pvpbot faction settings <faction> [word] the faction's screen, with what it changes marked"}, {"title": "Menus and the kit editor", "page": "Next update", "url": "next-update.html#menus-and-the-kit-editor", "text": "/pvpbot menu opens a hub of menus, and every button runs the matching /pvpbot command, so permissions and replies are the same as typing it. Bots: a head for each bot. Its panel opens its bag or ender chest live, gives it a kit, restocks it, sets its faction, sends it to follow or fight, pauses or removes it. Kits: edit, wear, hand out, delete, or make a new one (named in chat). Factions: members, kit odds, recruit bots nearby, follow, formation, war, peace and alliance, uniform, disband. Presets, settings and techniques. The kit editor lays a kit out like an inventory (bag, hotbar, armour, off-hand), with a second page for the kit's ender chest. Kits now keep every item component, so things like shield banners are no longer lost. /pvpbot menu /pvpbot kit edit <name> /pvpbot kit rename <old> <new> faction kit tables follow the new name"}, {"title": "Aim that decides hits", "page": "Next update", "url": "next-update.html#aim-that-decides-hits", "text": "A swing now lands where the crosshair is , the way a player's client picks what it hits, instead of \"in reach, then a dice roll\". The bot: sees you a moment late (the tracking knob); flicks onto you like a hand: a touch of overshoot, then it settles; carries a small tremor ( aim ) and aims at the upper body. It holds a ready swing until its crosshair is on you, for as long as its patience lasts. Walking a straight line or circling steadily gets you hit; a juke inside its reaction time makes it whiff. A whiff costs it the attack meter, as it does a player. Setting Default Values What it does aim-hits true true / false Its swing lands where its crosshair is: it aims a moment late, flicks and settles, and whiffs a juke (off: the old in-reach-and-a-dice-roll hit) aim-height 70 30 – 95 Where on the body it aims, percent of height from the feet - each bot a little higher or lower"}, {"title": "Senses", "page": "Next update", "url": "next-update.html#senses", "text": "View cone: someone behind a bot isn't seen until they're heard or they hit it. Hearing: sprinting (walking at half the range, crouching silent), fighting, a totem going off, eating and pearls make it stop and turn to look. A pursuer it is already fighting is tracked by ear. Blindness and Darkness blind it: it only notices someone within touching distance, though it remembers who it was fighting. Setting Default Values What it does fov 120 30 – 360 How wide it sees, in degrees: someone behind it is not seen until heard or felt (360 = all round) hearing 16 0 – 32 Blocks it hears sprinting, fighting, eating and pearls from, and turns to look (0 = deaf)"}, {"title": "Fighting smarter", "page": "Next update", "url": "next-update.html#fighting-smarter", "text": "Hotbar only: in a fight it uses its hotbar and off-hand. Anything in the bag means opening the inventory: it stands still, can't swing, and moves the item over. Between fights it refills its hotbar the way its kit laid it out. Damage model: it ranks weapons by the damage they do through your armour (toughness, Breach, Protection, Resistance, crits) and times heals by how fast it's losing health. It backs off sooner when losing the trade and stays in when winning. Reads you: how often you shield, jump-reset and strafe each way, when you pearl, how many totems and gapples you use. A shield player gets the axe every time, a jump-resetter gets crits, a pearl gets followed, and low with no totem showing means all in. Shield game: it drops its shield to a charged axe instead of feeding it, goes all out while yours is down, and keeps out of reach while its own is. Edges: it walks round to knock"}, {"title": "Playing like a person", "page": "Next update", "url": "next-update.html#playing-like-a-person", "text": "Personality: every bot gets its own habits from its name: how jumpy it is, how wide it strafes, the range it likes, rush or turtle, sprint-comboer or crit-hunter. Weak bots sometimes spam-click air, and pay for it. Spacing and whiffs: out of your reach while its meter refills, back in as it fills. It hovers at the edge of your reach to bait a swing, and punishes a whiff with a free hit. Tempo: it pushes after landing two in a row, backs off to reset after taking two, and takes a breath after a heavy trade. Items in hand first: gapples, potions and pearls come up a moment before use, and now and then the wrong slot comes up first. Panic or clutch: at three hearts a bot either locks in or falls apart. Better bots lock in more often. After a kill: some crouch over the body; most pick the totems, pearls, gapples and potions out of the drop. Fidgeting: standing about, bots look round, glance "}, {"title": "27 mace and spear techniques", "page": "Next update", "url": "next-update.html#27-mace-and-spear-techniques", "text": "Difficulty is how well a bot plays; techniques are which moves it knows at all. Each technique is an ordinary setting. The ones bots already used start on, the new ones start off. Core swaps: mace swap, spear-mace swap, breach swap, spear reach swap. Fundamentals: wind-charge mace, crouch boost, cooldown discipline (wind charges now carry vanilla's item cooldown), hit preference, hotbar shield tracking, elytra wind-charge boost. Movement: spear dash (a held charge and the spear on one click, distance picked by pitch), dash chain, spear-macing, wind-charge reset and self-reset. Anti-shield: ground and elytra backstab, stun slam (one-tick, easy or optimal), spear stun slam, shield drain. Finishers: detap spear-mace, detap breach, spear double pop, wind-charge cancel. Rocket mace with four climb-and-drop patterns, elytra spear , and a choice of pearl catch for the pearl launch. /pvpbot tech"}, {"title": "Survival, webs and escapes", "page": "Next update", "url": "next-update.html#survival-webs-and-escapes", "text": "Backing off to heal follows the open ground, not a straight line away. The bot looks round for the most open way out that still leads away from you (never into a wall or off a drop), sprint-jumps along it while there's room, and stops about ten blocks off. Cornered, it turns and fights instead of pressing into the wall. While a gapple or potion is up it eases back facing you, the way a player eats. Advanced healing: it buys a gap before it heals (a knockback hit, a web on you, or a short pearl) instead of eating in your face. healing: old brings back the previous behaviour. Off-hand preference (auto, totem or shield), a totem in the main hand when a smash is about to land, answers to being combo'd, and eating at three hunger bars. Webs are held out, looked at and swung, not a block appearing in a sword hand. They can predict where you walk, web your head against a wall, or knock you back"}, {"title": "Regearing", "page": "Next update", "url": "next-update.html#regearing", "text": "A bot regears from its ender chest the way it's done on an SMP: it pearls away, sets the chest down beside it, takes what it's short of (loose, or out of a shulker inside) and packs it all back. It waits for room first and recovers everything if you catch it mid-way. A shulker box in its bag works too, with no ender chest needed. It can also restock from a shulker box placed nearby, or mine yours out from under you. Every step is played out by hand, so you can watch it: the chest or box comes up in its hand and is placed with a swing and its sound, opened with the lid moving, emptied one stack at a time, closed, and mined with its best pickaxe over the real break time (everyone near sees the crack), and then the drop is picked up. How fast it goes follows skill. /pvpbot restock <bot|all|faction <name>> [instant] regear now /pvpbot view inventory|enderchest <bot> a bot's bag or ender ches"}, {"title": "More items", "page": "Next update", "url": "next-update.html#more-items", "text": "Each one only when it's on the hotbar: a fishing rod to reel a runner in, the trident (thrown, or ridden with Riptide), splash potions of Weakness, Slowness, Harming and Poison, firework crossbow ammo, the spear charge , and block clutches (slime, hay, cobweb, powder snow) when there's no water. Setting Default Values What it does fishing-rod true true / false Hooks you with a fishing rod and reels you in - off a ledge, toward lava, back from running trident true true / false Throws its trident (and waits for Loyalty to bring it back), rides Riptide in rain or water crossbow-ammo true true / false Loads firework rockets from its off-hand into a crossbow for burst damage throw-potions true true / false Throws Weakness, Slowness, Harming and Poison at you spear-charge false true / false Holds use with a spear as it runs in - the charged spear attack (check it on your version) block-clutch "}, {"title": "Formations, marches and uniforms", "page": "Next update", "url": "next-update.html#formations-marches-and-uniforms", "text": "Formations: behind , column , line , rows , triangle , wall and open_circle turn with the leader; square , circle , hexagon and grid line up with the world and hold their shape on arrival. Bots carrying an elytra form their own rank at the back. Marches: faction goto sends a whole faction to a point or a player in formation, then it holds, or attacks a target on arrival. Uniforms: faction skin puts every bot in a faction in one account's skin. It's kept in factions.yml , so it survives deaths and restarts. /pvpbot follow <bots> <target> [formation] [size] /pvpbot faction follow <faction> <target|stop> [formation] [size] /pvpbot faction goto <faction> <x y z|player|stop|status> [formation] [size] [then attack <target>|then hold] /pvpbot faction formation <faction> <shape> [size] | clear /pvpbot faction skin <faction> <account>|stop /pvpbot faction skin list"}, {"title": "Team play and armies", "page": "Next update", "url": "next-update.html#team-play-and-armies", "text": "Team play: focus fire on the enemy allies are on and the weakest one; peeling off to save a nearly dead ally; splash heals thrown at allies; regearing one at a time. Reaction ripple: orders and new targets reach each bot after its own moment, nearest first, so a side doesn't turn on one tick. Pile-on cap: no more than three on one enemy; the rest take the next or wait at the edge. Skill spread: each bot's skill sits a little above or below its faction's. Morale: a side that is outnumbered and losing members breaks, the least brave first. A leader close by holds them and rallies the ones that ran. Roles: archers hang back, pearl carriers flank, healers stay with the hurt. Loose formations and ally care : slots wobble, stragglers sprint-jump to catch up, and nobody shoots or sweeps through an ally. Setting Default Values What it does focus-fire true true / false In a faction: goes for the "}, {"title": "Arena and debug view", "page": "Next update", "url": "next-update.html#arena-and-debug-view", "text": "/pvpbot arena spawns two bots where you stand and has them fight round after round. It then reports per side: wins, time to kill, hits and damage per hit, aimed swings that connected, crits, smashes, best combo, totems popped, heals, and mid-fight inventory opens. Change a setting, run it again, compare. /pvpbot debugview floats a line over a bot showing which part of its AI is in charge, its target and how the trade looks, what it has read about you, its aim, and its latest call (\"whiffed\", \"their shield is down: all out\"). /pvpbot arena start <kitA> <kitB> [rounds] [skillA] [skillB] /pvpbot arena stop | report /pvpbot debugview <bot|all>"}, {"title": "/pvpbot diagnose works on players", "page": "Next update", "url": "next-update.html#pvpbot-diagnose-works-on-players", "text": "/pvpbot diagnose <bot> explains why a bot can't fight. The new form answers the opposite question: why bots can't hurt a particular player . /pvpbot diagnose <player> [reset] It reads the player's state the way the game's damage checks see it: game mode, the invulnerable flag, the \"client still loading\" protection, hit invulnerability, a raised shield, a shared scoreboard team, PvP in the world, and the client version. It lists every bot within 48 blocks, with whether it would fight them and whether it can see them. From server start, every bot hit aimed at a real player is logged, and the report shows what became of each one: Outcome What it means Turned away The game refused the hit before any plugin saw it. The reason is checked on the spot. Cancelled A plugin stopped it, and the report says at which event priority. Shield Their raised shield took it. Zeroed It went through with no da"}, {"title": "Changed behaviour", "page": "Next update", "url": "next-update.html#changed-behaviour", "text": "Sword crits: the cooldown trick swung at 84.8% charge, which loses vanilla's crit, sprint knockback and sweep (they need 90%). Bots now swing at 92%, so they combo properly. Fewer fake whiffs: strafing used to add up to 40 points of miss chance; it's capped at 15. Shield as a read: the shield goes up only when you can actually hit right now, not in every gap between the bot's own swings. Mace defence: alone under a mace player, a bot holds its ground near the landing spot ready to shield, and a dodge is a late sidestep. Wind-charge cancels are rare: 10% of launches, never twice in 15 seconds. Spear-macers actually smash: the spear dash now solves the velocity that lands on the target, and the smash is always swung with the mace. The opening walk-in ends once the gap has been growing for half a second, so a bot chases someone running. Followers keep up: a formation no longer falls behind "}, {"title": "Leaving PvpBot", "page": "Next update", "url": "next-update.html#leaving-pvpbot", "text": "/pvpbot tp rift is no longer part of PvpBot. tp portal and tp pearl stay."}, {"title": "For plugin developers", "page": "Next update", "url": "next-update.html#for-plugin-developers", "text": "An add-on API, BotAddons , lets another plugin teach bots to use its own gear and ways of getting about: guns, grenades, medical items, special armour, a lift or a launcher. With no add-on installed nothing changes. An add-on implements BotAddon (every hook defaults to \"not mine\") and is asked at fixed points in every bot's tick: Hook When override Right after the target is chosen, ahead of eating, pearls and retreats. fight With a target, ahead of every built-in attack. Returning false falls through to PvpBot's own play. idle With no target, ahead of guarding, escorting and wandering. route Whenever a bot walks somewhere, ahead of its own pathfinding: a transport the add-on runs. blocksSight Whenever a bot looks for a target: something only the add-on knows about (a smoke cloud) can hide a player. holdsHand , reserved and armourScore keep PvpBot's own auto-equip, bow code and armour pic"}, {"title": "Commands", "page": "Reference", "url": "commands.html", "text": "Every /pvpbot and /ghost command, grouped by topic. You need the pvpbot.admin permission (ops by default). /bot is an alias of /pvpbot . Values in < > are required, values in [ ] are optional, and | separates alternatives. Wherever it says <selector> you can use a name, all , latest , faction <name> , overfaction <name> or random[...] : see Selectors . Coming in the next update difficulty , playstyle , techniques , restock , view , arena , debugview , menu , kit edit and kit rename , the settings menu, faction goto , formation and skin , and diagnose <player> . They're on Next update ."}, {"title": "Managing bots", "page": "Commands", "url": "commands.html#managing-bots", "text": "Command What it does /pvpbot spawn Spawns a bot with a generated name where you stand (from the console: at the first world's spawn). /pvpbot spawn <name> Spawns a bot with that name (16 characters at most). If an account has that name the bot wears its skin, otherwise a random one. /pvpbot spawn <name> <skinFrom> Spawns a bot wearing the skin of the account skinFrom . /pvpbot spawn <name> none Spawns the bot at once, without looking up a skin. Useful in scripts, where the next line must already find the bot. /pvpbot spawn <count> Spawns up to 50 bots with generated names. /pvpbot spawn names:<table> <count> [skinFrom] Spawns bots with names taken from a table in config.yml . With skinFrom the whole batch wears the same skin. /pvpbot remove <bot> Removes a bot. /pvpbot removeall Removes every bot. /pvpbot list Lists the active bots. /pvpbot reload Reloads config.yml and the other files."}, {"title": "Combat orders", "page": "Commands", "url": "commands.html#combat-orders", "text": "Command What it does /pvpbot attack <bot> <target> The bot hunts a player or another bot, by name. /pvpbot fight <bot> <bot> Two bots fight each other, even with bot-vs-bot off. /pvpbot stop <bot> Clears the bot's orders and stops it fighting. /pvpbot retreat <selector> [to <player|x y z>] Fall back. Without to every bot runs its own way; with to they regroup on a player (followed as they move) or at coordinates. Outranks follow, guard and attack until called off. /pvpbot retreat stop <selector> Calls off the retreat. /pvpbot hide <selector> The bot hides: with an invisibility potion it drinks it and takes its armour off, without one it crouches. It stops starting fights but keeps following orders, and comes out by itself when an enemy is close and can see it. /pvpbot hide stop <selector> Brings the bot out of hiding. /pvpbot tether <target> <leader> <selector> [radius] A leash: the bots"}, {"title": "Movement and positioning", "page": "Commands", "url": "commands.html#movement-and-positioning", "text": "Command What it does /pvpbot follow <selector> <target> [formation] Escort a player or a bot. Formations: behind (default), circle , open_circle . Followers fight when attacked, then fall back in. /pvpbot follow stop <bot|all> Stop following. /pvpbot guard <bot|all|faction <name>> <radius> [x y z] Hold a spot (your position or the coordinates) within a radius of 2 to 128 blocks (default 12). Attacks anyone who comes in, and walks back to its post if a fight pulls it too far. Alias: protect . /pvpbot guard stop [bot|all] Stands the guards down. /pvpbot pause <bot|all|faction <name>> Suspends the AI: the bot stays in the world, falls and takes damage, but does nothing. With no argument, lists the paused bots. /pvpbot resume <bot|all|faction <name>> Turns the AI back on. Alias: unpause . /pvpbot tp <bot|all|faction <name>> <player|bot|x y z> Instant teleport."}, {"title": "Squad teleport", "page": "Commands", "url": "commands.html#squad-teleport", "text": "Three showy ways to bring a group somewhere. range is how far around the destination the bots are scattered (default 100, at most 1000). Command What it does /pvpbot tp portal [ground] <selector> <player|bot|x y z> [range] Opens real nether portals (obsidian frame, lit inside) around the destination and has the bots step out: up to 3 per portal, which may float; with ground portals always sit on the ground and up to 5 share one. The portals stay and never lead to the Nether. /pvpbot tp pearl <selector> <player|bot|x y z> [range] Each bot arrives on a real ender pearl thrown from high above: real flight, landing and a little fall damage. /pvpbot tp rift [big] <selector> <player|bot|x y z> [range] Leaving Tears in reality that close right behind the bots: up to 5 per rift, or one big shared rift with big ."}, {"title": "Patrol routes", "page": "Commands", "url": "commands.html#patrol-routes", "text": "You build a route by walking it: create the route, then add a point at each spot you stop at. Command What it does /pvpbot path create <name> Creates an empty route. /pvpbot path add <name> Adds your position as the next point (in-game only). /pvpbot path remove <name> <index> Removes a point. /pvpbot path delete <name> Deletes the route and stops anyone walking it. /pvpbot path list [name] Lists the routes, or one route's points. /pvpbot path assign <selector> <route> Sends the bots to patrol the route. /pvpbot path stop <selector> Stops the patrol."}, {"title": "Settings", "page": "Commands", "url": "commands.html#settings", "text": "Command What it does /pvpbot set <setting> <value> Changes a global setting, live and for good (it is written to config.yml ). /pvpbot settings [group|text] Shows the current values, by group ( general , combat , movement , utility , ranged , building , detection ) or filtered by name. * marks a default not yet written to the file. /pvpbot faction set <faction> <setting> <value> Changes a setting for one faction only. /pvpbot preset [name] With no name, shows the active preset and the ones available; with a name, applies it. /pvpbot preset save <name> Saves the values in force right now as a preset (overwrites if it exists). /pvpbot preset delete <name> Deletes a preset. /pvpbot preset off Turns the preset off: back to config.yml as written."}, {"title": "Factions", "page": "Commands", "url": "commands.html#factions", "text": "Alias: /pvpbot f . Details on Factions . Command What it does /pvpbot faction create <name> Creates a faction. /pvpbot faction disband <name> Disbands a faction. /pvpbot faction join <faction> <member|selector> Adds a player or bots. /pvpbot faction join <faction> range <blocks> Adds every bot within that distance of you (0–512). /pvpbot faction leave <member> Removes a member from their faction. /pvpbot faction hostile <a> <b> War: they attack each other on sight. /pvpbot faction peace <a> <b> Peace: neither side starts anything, but both hit back. /pvpbot faction ally <a> <b> Alliance: the same side, each with its own settings. /pvpbot faction unally <a> <b> Ends the alliance. /pvpbot faction leader <faction> <player|bot|clear> Sets or clears the leader. /pvpbot faction follow <faction> <target|stop> [formation] The whole faction follows someone. /pvpbot faction kit <faction> set <kit>"}, {"title": "Overfactions", "page": "Commands", "url": "commands.html#overfactions", "text": "Coalitions of factions. Alias: /pvpbot of . Command What it does /pvpbot overfaction create|disband <name> Creates or disbands a coalition. /pvpbot overfaction add <overfaction> <faction> Adds a faction: it becomes allied with every other one inside. /pvpbot overfaction remove <faction> Takes a faction out of its coalition. /pvpbot overfaction hostile|peace <a> <b> War or peace between two coalitions, or a coalition and a single faction, in one go. /pvpbot overfaction leader <overfaction> <player|bot|clear> A leader for the whole coalition. /pvpbot overfaction follow <overfaction> <target|stop> [formation] The whole coalition follows someone. /pvpbot overfaction tp <overfaction> <player|bot|x y z> Teleports the whole coalition. /pvpbot overfaction info|list Info and list."}, {"title": "Kits", "page": "Commands", "url": "commands.html#kits", "text": "Command What it does /pvpbot kit save <name> [bot] Saves your inventory as a kit, or a bot's. /pvpbot kit give <kit> <player|selector> Gives the kit to a player (real ones too) or to bots. /pvpbot kit givefaction <kit> <faction|selector> Gives the kit to every bot in a faction. /pvpbot kit delete <name> Deletes a kit. /pvpbot kit list Lists the kits."}, {"title": "Scripts", "page": "Commands", "url": "commands.html#scripts", "text": "Details on Scripts . Command What it does /pvpbot script create <name> Creates an empty script. /pvpbot script add <name> <command> Adds a line (no leading slash). {1} … {9} are the parameters, wait <ticks> is a pause. /pvpbot script show <name> Shows the numbered lines. /pvpbot script remove <name> <line> Removes a line. /pvpbot script delete <name> Deletes the script. /pvpbot script run <name> [args...] Runs the script as if you typed it. /pvpbot script list Lists the scripts."}, {"title": "Companions", "page": "Commands", "url": "commands.html#companions", "text": "Details on Companions . Command What it does /pvpbot owner set <bot> <player> Hands the bot to an online player, who can then command it in chat. /pvpbot owner clear <bot> Removes the owner. /pvpbot owner list <player> A player's bots. /pvpbot owner help Summary of the chat orders."}, {"title": "Diagnostics and tools", "page": "Commands", "url": "commands.html#diagnostics-and-tools", "text": "Command What it does /pvpbot diagnose <bot> Works out why a bot can't fight (login plugins, invulnerability, damage cancelled by another plugin) and suggests the fix. /pvpbot diagnose <player> [reset] Next update Why bots won't fight or can't hurt a real player: their state, every bot nearby, and what became of each bot hit aimed at them. /pvpbot debug <bot> The bot's internal state: awareness, building, clutch, faction. /pvpbot debug <bot> invulnerable <true|false> Forces the vanilla invulnerable flag, to reproduce a problem. /pvpbot givebow <bot> Gives the bot a bow and arrows. /pvpbot drop <bot> <item id> [amount] Drops an item at the bot's feet (vanilla id, e.g. diamond_sword ). /pvpbot resetattr <player|bot> Removes every attribute modifier left by other plugins (bosses, items, roles) and restores the base values. Alias: resetattributes . /pvpbot clearplayerdata Deletes the player f"}, {"title": "Ghosts", "page": "Commands", "url": "commands.html#ghosts", "text": "Details on Ghosts . Command What it does /ghost record <name> Starts recording your movement (and the blocks you break or place). /ghost stoprecord Stops and saves the recording. /ghost spawn <name> <recording> [skin:<name>|skin:none] [line1|line2|...] Spawns a ghost that replays the recording forever. /ghost stare <name> [skin:<name>|skin:none] [line1|line2|...] Spawns a still ghost that stares at the nearest player and vanishes when someone comes close. /ghost remove <name> Removes a ghost. /ghost list Lists the ghosts. /ghost recordings Lists the recordings."}, {"title": "Selectors", "page": "Reference", "url": "selectors.html", "text": "How to point one command at one bot, many bots or all of them. Most orders take a selector in place of a bot's name. It works the same everywhere: follow , retreat , hide , tether , tp portal/pearl/rift , path assign/stop , faction join , kit give and kit givefaction . The command list writes it as <selector> ."}, {"title": "List", "page": "Selectors", "url": "selectors.html#list", "text": "Selector Who it picks <name> One bot, by name. all Every active bot. latest Every bot from the last spawn command that is still around. faction <name> A faction's active bots. overfaction <name> or of <name> The active bots of every faction in a coalition. random One bot at random. random[filters] Random bots that pass every filter (see below). list:a,b,c An explicit list of names. Mostly used by scripts . Commands with fewer options guard , pause , resume and the instant tp only take <bot> , all or faction <name> . follow stop and guard stop take a name or all ."}, {"title": "random[...]", "page": "Selectors", "url": "selectors.html#random", "text": "Picks at random among the bots that pass every filter. Filters are key=value pairs separated by commas; spaces after the commas are fine. Filter Meaning limit=<n> How many bots at most (default 1). nofaction=true|false Only bots with no faction, or only bots in one. paused=true|false Only paused bots, or only running ones. world=<name> Only bots in that world. alreadyhaskit=true|false Only bots already carrying something, or only empty-handed ones. alreadyhaskit is an approximation The plugin doesn't record which kit a bot was given, so the filter only checks whether the inventory is empty. A bot that picked an item up off the ground counts as \"already has a kit\"."}, {"title": "Examples", "page": "Selectors", "url": "selectors.html#examples", "text": "/pvpbot kit give iron random[limit=5,alreadyhaskit=false] /pvpbot faction join bandits random[limit=3, nofaction=true] /pvpbot tp pearl random[limit=10,world=world] Steve 30 /pvpbot follow faction red Steve circle /pvpbot retreat overfaction north to 100 64 -250 /pvpbot hide latest"}, {"title": "Settings", "page": "Reference", "url": "settings.html", "text": "Every option that changes how bots behave, with its default and limits. Coming in the next update 98 more settings, for difficulty knobs, playstyles, aim, senses, tactics, techniques and more. They're listed with their defaults on Next update ."}, {"title": "Changing settings", "page": "Settings", "url": "settings.html#changing-settings", "text": "In game: /pvpbot set <name> <value> . Applies to every bot at once and is written to config.yml . For one faction: /pvpbot faction set <faction> <name> <value> . Applies only to that faction's bots. In the file: edit plugins/PvpBot/config.yml , then run /pvpbot reload . To see them: /pvpbot settings , or /pvpbot settings combat for one group, or /pvpbot settings pearl to filter by name. Values outside the limits are refused. Times are in ticks (20 ticks = 1 second), distances in blocks, and health in half-hearts (20 = full health)."}, {"title": "Which value wins", "page": "Settings", "url": "settings.html#which-value-wins", "text": "When a bot reads a setting, the most specific layer wins: config.yml (or the default, if the key isn't there); the active preset , if any; the bot's faction settings. Active preset If the active preset sets the same key, /pvpbot set writes it to config.yml but the preset's value still wins. The command tells you so: use /pvpbot preset off , or save the preset again with /pvpbot preset save <name> ."}, {"title": "General", "page": "Settings", "url": "settings.html#general", "text": "Group general : periodic checks, restarts and compatibility with other plugins. Setting Default Values What it does check-interval 20 1 – 200 Ticks between equipment checks (best armour and weapon). bots-relog true true / false Bring bots back after a server restart. force-vulnerable true true / false Clear the invulnerable flag on bots. Login plugins set it and never clear it. auth-autologin true true / false Register bots with the login plugin (AuthMe and similar) so it stops freezing them. bypass-damage-protection false true / false Un-cancel damage involving bots. Overrides region and PvP protection, so use with care. target-scan-interval 10 1 – 60 Ticks between target searches. Raise it if a large crowd of bots lags."}, {"title": "Combat", "page": "Settings", "url": "settings.html#combat", "text": "Group combat : who to attack, how, and with which techniques. Longer explanations on Combat . Setting Default Values What it does auto-target true true / false Hunt the nearest enemy unprompted. target-players true true / false Pick players as targets unprompted (off = mobs only, unless ordered or hit). target-passive-mobs false true / false Also pick passive and neutral mobs as targets (off = hostile mobs only). revenge true true / false Fight back against whoever hits the bot. friendly-fire false true / false Allow hitting allies. bot-vs-bot false true / false Bots pick fights with each other unprompted. attack-invincible false true / false Also attack players in creative or spectator mode. max-target-distance 32.0 1 – 128 How far (in blocks) a bot looks for targets. melee-range 3.0 1 – 6 Reach, from the eyes to the hitbox as vanilla measures it (3.0 = a player's). melee-standoff 2.2 0"}, {"title": "Movement", "page": "Settings", "url": "settings.html#movement", "text": "Group movement . Setting Default Values What it does bhop true true / false Jump constantly while chasing. speed 1.0 0.1 – 2 Movement speed (1.0 = sprint). wander true true / false Walk around when idle. follow-distance 3.5 1 – 20 How far a follower stays from whoever it escorts."}, {"title": "Utility and survival", "page": "Settings", "url": "settings.html#utility-and-survival", "text": "Group utility : gear, healing, totems, pearls, cobwebs, water. Setting Default Values What it does auto-equip true true / false Wear the best armour and hold the best weapon carried. auto-repair true true / false Collect nearby XP orbs and mend damaged gear with them. auto-repair-bottles true true / false Once nearby orbs run out, crack carried XP bottles to mend gear. auto-totem true true / false Grab a totem when close to death. totem-health 8.0 0 – 20 Health at which the totem goes into the hand. retreat true true / false Back off when low on health. retreat-health 6.0 0 – 20 Health at which to retreat. auto-food true true / false Eat when hungry. food-threshold 16 0 – 20 Food level below which to eat. auto-shield true true / false Raise a shield when hurt. shield-health 8.0 0 – 20 Health at which to start blocking. auto-potion true true / false Drink healing potions and eat golden ap"}, {"title": "Ranged", "page": "Settings", "url": "settings.html#ranged", "text": "Group ranged : bows and crossbows. Setting Default Values What it does ranged true true / false Use bows and crossbows. ranged-min-range 6.0 0 – 64 Closer than this, switch to melee. ranged-optimal-range 12.0 1 – 64 Distance to hold while shooting. bow-min-draw-ticks 18 1 – 60 Draw time before releasing (20 = full draw). arrow-prediction true true / false Lead shots ahead of moving targets. ranged-shot-gap 25 0 – 200 Fewest ticks between one arrow and the next draw. ranged-volley-size 3 1 – 20 Most arrows in a row before lowering the bow (each volley is 1 to this many). ranged-rest-ticks 80 0 – 600 How long the bot stops shooting after a volley, closing in meanwhile."}, {"title": "Building", "page": "Settings", "url": "settings.html#building", "text": "Group building : what bots may do to the world's blocks. They only ever break soft natural blocks (dirt, sand, gravel, clay, leaves, undergrowth, cobwebs), never stone, ore, builds or containers. Setting Default Values What it does building true true / false Let bots bridge, pillar and dig through soft cover (never stone or anything built). building-bridge true true / false Place a block to cross a gap. building-pillar true true / false Tower up toward a target that is above and out of reach. building-clear-soft true true / false Dig through dirt, sand, gravel, leaves and undergrowth in the way. building-cooldown 6 0 – 200 Ticks between block actions. building-budget 64 0 – 2000 Most blocks a bot may place and break per minute. building-max-pillar 6 0 – 64 Tallest tower a bot will build."}, {"title": "Detection", "page": "Settings", "url": "settings.html#detection", "text": "Group detection : when a bot notices a target. The multipliers scale the distance you're noticed at: below 1 means you have to be closer to be seen. Setting Default Values What it does detection true true / false Bots must actually see a target to know about it (off = the old plain-radius sweep). detection-touch-radius 3.0 0 – 16 Within this distance a target is noticed even without line of sight. detection-memory-ticks 100 0 – 1200 How long a spotted target stays known after leaving line of sight. detection-invisible 0.15 0 – 2 Notice-range multiplier against an invisible player (0.15 = spotted at 15% of the usual distance). detection-sneaking 0.5 0 – 2 Multiplier against a crouching player. detection-armoured 1.0 0 – 2 Multiplier against a player wearing armour. detection-unarmoured 0.7 0 – 2 Multiplier against a player wearing none."}, {"title": "Other parts of config.yml", "page": "Settings", "url": "settings.html#other-parts-of-config-yml", "text": "These parts of config.yml can't be changed with /pvpbot set : edit the file, then run /pvpbot reload . Key Default What it does active-preset '' The active preset. Changed with /pvpbot preset . presets casual, normal, sweaty, passive The saved presets. See Presets . skins.random missing missing , always or off . See Skins and names . skins.built-in-pool true Use the few hundred skins that ship with the plugin. skins.pool [] Extra account names whose skins bots can wear. name-tables default, bandits Name lists for /pvpbot spawn names:<table> . companions.holding-world test_world The world companions are parked in while their owner is offline. See Companions ."}, {"title": "Combat", "page": "Features", "url": "combat.html", "text": "How a bot picks targets, how it hits, and which techniques it uses."}, {"title": "A human opponent", "page": "Combat", "url": "combat.html#a-human-opponent", "text": "PvpBot is built for training and recording, not for winning at all costs. No move is frame-perfect: skill (0–100, default 50) sets the speed of the reactions, the timing and the aim of every technique. Mace smashes and elytra hits land a little early or late; crit hops are sometimes mistimed. The axe against a shield comes after the shield is seen; the shield against a smash goes up a reaction late. Weapon-swap combos fumble now and then. Practised moves, like the mace stun or the pearl launch, come off most of the time. Arrows and pearls are a little off and react late to a juke. Even at 100 there is a gap to play around. Reach is a player's ( melee-range: 3.0 , measured like vanilla, from the eyes to the hitbox), aim turns at a limited speed ( turn-speed ) and ping-millis simulates a real connection's latency. On top of that come miss-chance (swings missed on purpose) and mistake-chanc"}, {"title": "Picking a target", "page": "Combat", "url": "combat.html#picking-a-target", "text": "auto-target : the bot looks for the nearest enemy within max-target-distance by itself. target-players and target-passive-mobs decide whether players and passive mobs count. revenge : it hits back at whoever hits it. bot-vs-bot : bots attack each other without orders. Factions decide who is an enemy, an ally or at peace. priority-hp-target : at equal priority it prefers the target with the least health."}, {"title": "Detection", "page": "Combat", "url": "combat.html#detection", "text": "With detection on, a bot only knows about a target if: it can see it; it is very close ( detection-touch-radius ); or it was told about it by an order. After losing sight it remembers the target for detection-memory-ticks . Crouching, being invisible or wearing no armour shortens the distance you're noticed at."}, {"title": "It doesn't attack at once", "page": "Combat", "url": "combat.html#it-doesn-t-attack-at-once", "text": "A new bot leaves players alone for spawn-grace seconds. When it notices a player by itself, it sizes them up for reaction-ticks before attacking. A hit, an order or a faction war is answered at once."}, {"title": "Melee", "page": "Combat", "url": "combat.html#melee", "text": "Swords, axes and spears. The bot keeps a fighting distance ( melee-standoff ) instead of walking into you, and swings every attack-cooldown ticks. The techniques, each switchable on its own: Setting Technique crits , crit-chain Crits from a real jump, back to back if wanted. cooldown-trick Swings at 84.8% cooldown: full damage and knockback, but sooner. hit-select Waits for a favourable angle and spacing. d-tap Sideways sprint reset after a hit. mirror-punish Hits back mid-air off your own knockback. sweep-hit Sweep attack when 2 or more enemies are on it. shield-disable Switches to an axe against a raised shield ( shield-disable-chance of the time). shield-read-windup Raises the shield when it reads your crit windup."}, {"title": "Mace", "page": "Combat", "url": "combat.html#mace", "text": "With mace on, the bot uses the mace with tactics, not at every chance: Launch and smash: it launches with a wind charge or a jump only when the smash can land: close enough, shield down, target not running, and a few seconds between launches. Pearl launch ( mace-pearl-launch ): throws a pearl and pops a wind charge on it mid-flight, going far higher. Not always the mace: special-weapon-melee-chance of the time it fights with an ordinary weapon. Wind Burst: a smash with a Wind Burst mace throws it back up for the next one ( jump-reset chains smashes). Defence: against an incoming smash it raises a shield ( mace-defence-shield ), gets out of the way ( mace-defence-flee ) or takes it. mace-totem-chance of the time it keeps a totem in the offhand instead of a shield."}, {"title": "Elytra mace", "page": "Combat", "url": "combat.html#elytra-mace", "text": "With elytra-mace (needs elytra, rockets and a mace) the bot: climbs on rockets above the fight, to elytra-mace-height blocks; dives at where you're heading; smashes without landing: it drops the glide for the swing and picks it up again straight after, as a player does; climbs back up for another pass. A last-moment sidestep gets out of the way. With no rockets it uses whatever height it has: if Wind Burst, a wind charge, a pearl or a drop takes it up high, it opens the elytra, glides over you and dives."}, {"title": "Cart PvP", "page": "Combat", "url": "combat.html#cart-pvp", "text": "With cart-pvp (needs a Flame bow, arrows, rails and TNT minecarts) the bot: draws the bow and looses; puts a rail and a TNT minecart in the arrow's path; the arrow sets the cart off at your feet. Before doing it, it works out the damage with vanilla's own explosion formula (distance, blocks in the way, armour, Protection, difficulty). It only goes ahead if: the trade favours it; its average damage taken is under cart-max-self-damage ; not even the worst roll can kill it (unless it holds a totem); no ally is in the blast."}, {"title": "Weapon swapping", "page": "Combat", "url": "combat.html#weapon-swapping", "text": "attribute-swap is the master switch for three techniques: breach-swap : hits with a sword carrying the mace's Breach/Density enchantments; mace-stun : an axe to break the shield, then the mace straight after; spear-lunge : chained spear jabs to close distance (needs Lunge). The rate limit is swap-cooldown ."}, {"title": "Bows and crossbows", "page": "Combat", "url": "combat.html#bows-and-crossbows", "text": "Holds ranged-optimal-range and switches to melee under ranged-min-range . With arrow-prediction it leads moving targets. Fires in volleys ( ranged-volley-size ), then stops for ranged-rest-ticks and closes in. Against mace users: if it saw you holding a mace in the last few seconds, it loads a Slow Falling arrow first."}, {"title": "Survival", "page": "Combat", "url": "combat.html#survival", "text": "Totems ( auto-totem ): into the hand under totem-health , after a short pause ( totem-delay-ticks ). Food, shield, potions: eats without waste, blocks when hurt, drinks healing potions and eats golden apples. It won't use an enchanted apple while it has a plain one. Buff potions ( buff-potions ) only when there's actually a fight. Retreat ( retreat ): backs off when low. Pearls ( pearls ): to escape when nearly dead or to chase someone running away. Cobwebs ( cobwebs ): cobweb-global-cooldown stops a squad from chain-webbing you. Water: an MLG bucket on long falls ( water-clutch ), scoops up the water you place ( drain ), and when webbed tries to escape with water, sometimes fumbling. Repair ( auto-repair ): mends gear with Mending by collecting XP. Enemy stashes ( break-stashes ): breaks the shulker box or ender chest you place mid-fight."}, {"title": "Other", "page": "Combat", "url": "combat.html#other", "text": "Orbital Strike Cannon ( orbital ): if the bot has the datapack's rods, it uses them without blowing itself up. It won't fire at anyone inside the blast radius, runs after firing, and holds fire if an ally is in the area. Stasis ( stasis , off by default): blocks a killing hit and pulls the bot away like a pearl, healing it to half health. It's used per faction ( /pvpbot faction set bosses stasis true ) for characters that must not die."}, {"title": "Factions", "page": "Features", "url": "factions.html", "text": "Who fights whom: factions, relations, leaders, coalitions and per-faction settings."}, {"title": "The basics", "page": "Factions", "url": "factions.html#the-basics", "text": "A faction is a group of members: bots and real players. Everyone is in one faction at a time; joining another means leaving the old one. /pvpbot faction create bandits /pvpbot faction join bandits latest /pvpbot faction join bandits Steve /pvpbot faction join bandits range 20 /pvpbot faction leave Steve /pvpbot faction info bandits join takes a name, a selector or range <blocks> , which adds every bot within that distance of you."}, {"title": "The \"player\" faction", "page": "Factions", "url": "factions.html#the-player-faction", "text": "Every real player who joins the server without a faction is put in the player faction. Anyone you put in another faction (or made a leader) stays there after reconnecting. This lets you declare war or peace on every player in one go: /pvpbot faction hostile bandits player"}, {"title": "Relations", "page": "Factions", "url": "factions.html#relations", "text": "Relation Command Effect Neutral (none) Normal behaviour: a bot with auto-target attacks anyone, as if there were no factions. War hostile <a> <b> They attack each other on sight. Ends any alliance or peace. Peace peace <a> <b> Neither side starts anything. Blasts and sweeps treat them as bystanders. A member who is hit still hits back, and the leader's call to arms still counts. Alliance ally <a> <b> The same side in everything: they don't target each other, no revenge between them, orbital strikes hold fire near an ally. Each faction keeps its own settings. War and peace rule each other out. unally ends an alliance. An alliance or one faction? An alliance puts bots of different calibre on the same side: bandits and elite_bandits fight together but with different skill ."}, {"title": "Leaders", "page": "Factions", "url": "factions.html#leaders", "text": "/pvpbot faction leader bandits Boss /pvpbot faction leader bandits clear The leader (a bot or a real player) is always a member of the faction. The whole faction joins the leader's fights: whoever the leader attacks, and whoever attacks the leader, becomes everyone's target. The hostility lasts leader-call-seconds seconds (30 by default, 0 turns it off) and doesn't survive a restart. A leader who logs out is still the leader when they come back."}, {"title": "Following as a group", "page": "Factions", "url": "factions.html#following-as-a-group", "text": "/pvpbot faction follow bandits Boss circle /pvpbot faction follow bandits stop Formation Layout behind Behind whoever they follow (default). circle A ring all around. open_circle A ring that leaves the front clear. The spacing is follow-distance . Followers fight when attacked, then fall back in."}, {"title": "Per-faction settings", "page": "Factions", "url": "factions.html#per-faction-settings", "text": "Any setting can be changed for one faction only. Whatever the faction doesn't set comes from the active preset or from config.yml . /pvpbot faction set rookies skill 20 /pvpbot faction set rookies miss-chance 40 /pvpbot faction set veterans skill 90 /pvpbot faction set veterans elytra-mace true Faction settings always beat the preset: if you change the server's preset, the factions you tuned by hand stay as you left them."}, {"title": "Random kits per faction", "page": "Factions", "url": "factions.html#random-kits-per-faction", "text": "A faction can have a weighted table of kits. give rolls a random kit for each bot using those odds. /pvpbot faction kit bandits set diamond 30 /pvpbot faction kit bandits set mace 50 /pvpbot faction kit bandits set crystal 20 /pvpbot faction kit bandits list /pvpbot faction kit bandits give Weights are relative (here 30%, 50%, 20%) and go from 0 to 1000. A weight of 0 removes the kit from the table, and clear empties it."}, {"title": "Overfactions", "page": "Factions", "url": "factions.html#overfactions", "text": "An overfaction is a coalition of factions. Every faction inside is automatically allied with all the others: six factions is one command, not fifteen ally calls. /pvpbot overfaction create north /pvpbot overfaction add north bandits /pvpbot overfaction add north pirates /pvpbot overfaction create south /pvpbot overfaction add south guards /pvpbot overfaction hostile north south hostile and peace take two overfactions, or an overfaction and a single faction. overfaction <name> works as a selector everywhere: you can move, retreat or kit out a whole coalition with one command. leader , follow and tp work as they do for factions. An overfaction's leader doesn't join any faction and doesn't survive a restart."}, {"title": "Kits and presets", "page": "Features", "url": "kits-and-presets.html", "text": "Saved loadouts to hand to bots, and behaviour profiles to apply to the whole server in one command."}, {"title": "Kits", "page": "Kits and presets", "url": "kits-and-presets.html#kits", "text": "A kit is a snapshot of an inventory: hotbar, inventory, armour and offhand. Enchantments, names and item components all survive. /pvpbot kit save diamond /pvpbot kit save mace Marco /pvpbot kit list /pvpbot kit delete diamond The first command saves your inventory, the second saves the bot Marco 's."}, {"title": "Giving a kit", "page": "Kits and presets", "url": "kits-and-presets.html#giving-a-kit", "text": "/pvpbot kit give diamond Marco /pvpbot kit give diamond Steve /pvpbot kit give diamond latest /pvpbot kit givefaction diamond bandits give takes a bot, a real player (handy to spar on equal terms) or a selector . givefaction takes a faction name or a selector. The kit replaces whatever the receiver was carrying. To hand out different kits at random inside a faction, use per-faction kit tables . Moving kits.yml between servers Items are saved in the format of the server that created them. A kits.yml copied from a server on a different build may fail to load: PvpBot then starts without kits and says so in the console. Make the kits again on the new server."}, {"title": "Presets", "page": "Kits and presets", "url": "kits-and-presets.html#presets", "text": "A preset is a named set of settings applied to every bot on the server in one go. /pvpbot preset /pvpbot preset sweaty /pvpbot preset off With no name it shows the active preset and the ones available. With a name it applies it. off goes back to config.yml as written. A preset sits above config.yml but below faction settings. A faction you tuned by hand stays as it is when you change preset."}, {"title": "Built-in presets", "page": "Kits and presets", "url": "kits-and-presets.html#built-in-presets", "text": "Preset For What it sets casual Beginners: slow, sloppy bots. skill 25, miss-chance 30, mistake-chance 35, attack-cooldown 18, reaction-ticks 60; no crits, pearls or cobwebs. normal The default behaviour, as a preset. skill 50, miss-chance 10, mistake-chance 12, attack-cooldown 12, reaction-ticks 30, crits. sweaty Everything on and sharp, but still human. skill 85, miss-chance 3, mistake-chance 5, attack-cooldown 10, ping 25 ms, reaction-ticks 8; crits, axe against shields, buff potions, cobwebs, pearl launch, weapon swapping, elytra mace and cart PvP. passive Scenes and training dummies. They don't look for targets, don't wander and don't fight other bots; they only hit back when hit. Anything a preset doesn't name comes from config.yml ."}, {"title": "Making one", "page": "Kits and presets", "url": "kits-and-presets.html#making-one", "text": "/pvpbot set skill 70 /pvpbot set pearls false /pvpbot preset save tournament /pvpbot preset tournament preset save saves the values in force right now , including any preset already active. To edit a preset: apply it, change what you want with /pvpbot set , and save it again under the same name. /pvpbot preset delete <name> deletes it. Presets live in config.yml under presets: and can be written by hand too, using the same names as /pvpbot set ."}, {"title": "Movement and orders", "page": "Features", "url": "movement.html", "text": "How bots get around, what they may do to blocks, and the orders that position them."}, {"title": "How they walk", "page": "Movement and orders", "url": "movement.html#how-they-walk", "text": "Bots don't teleport in small steps: they press \"keys\" like a player and let vanilla physics do the rest. That's why they collide, take knockback and fall like a real player. A* pathfinding around obstacles, weighted to find long detours too (round a wide wall, out of a U-shaped trap). Straight line: when the way is clear it walks straight at the target, so it tracks someone moving tick by tick instead of following a stale route. Smoothed routes: it cuts across open ground and faces where it walks. It walks off planned drops instead of bridging over them, climbs ladders down as well as up, and plans round closed iron doors. Bunny hop ( bhop ) only on long straight stretches, with each bot on its own rhythm so a squad doesn't hop in step. Boats: boards a boat, places one if needed and steers it across water. Wandering ( wander ): walks around when there's nothing to do. speed sets the pace"}, {"title": "Building", "page": "Movement and orders", "url": "movement.html#building", "text": "With building on, a bot can: bridge a gap ( building-bridge ) with the blocks it carries, or a wind charge if it has none; pillar up toward a target above and out of reach ( building-pillar , at most building-max-pillar blocks); dig through soft cover ( building-clear-soft ): dirt, sand, gravel, clay, leaves, undergrowth, cobwebs. It never breaks stone, ore, builds or containers. Every change goes through the normal events, so WorldGuard, GriefPrevention, claim plugins and block loggers see it and can refuse it. The pace is limited by building-cooldown and building-budget (blocks per minute)."}, {"title": "Following", "page": "Movement and orders", "url": "movement.html#following", "text": "/pvpbot follow Marco Steve /pvpbot follow latest Steve circle /pvpbot follow stop all Formations: behind , circle , open_circle (see Factions ). It's an escort, not a leash: followers fight when attacked, then fall back in."}, {"title": "Guarding", "page": "Movement and orders", "url": "movement.html#guarding", "text": "/pvpbot guard faction guards 15 /pvpbot guard Marco 10 100 64 -200 /pvpbot guard stop all The bots hold a spot (your position, or the given coordinates) and attack anyone who enters the radius. Each patrols its own part of the perimeter. If a fight pulls them too far, they break off and walk back to their post, so they don't chase someone into a cave and get lost."}, {"title": "Patrols", "page": "Movement and orders", "url": "movement.html#patrols", "text": "/pvpbot path create walls /pvpbot path add walls /pvpbot path add walls /pvpbot path add walls /pvpbot path assign faction guards walls Each path add adds the spot you're standing on, so you build the route by walking it. At the last point the bots turn back instead of jumping to the first. path list walls shows the points, path remove walls 2 removes one, and path stop ends the patrol."}, {"title": "Retreat", "page": "Movement and orders", "url": "movement.html#retreat", "text": "/pvpbot retreat faction bandits /pvpbot retreat all to Steve /pvpbot retreat stop all Without to each bot runs its own way, so the squad scatters. With to they regroup on a player (following them as they move) or at coordinates. A retreat outranks follow, guard and attack. The bot only hits back at whoever just hit it or is right on top of it. It lasts until you call it off, or until you give stop or a new attack . The automatic low-health retreat is set with retreat and retreat-health ."}, {"title": "Hiding", "page": "Movement and orders", "url": "movement.html#hiding", "text": "/pvpbot hide latest /pvpbot hide stop latest With an invisibility potion (drinkable or splash): it uses it and takes off its armour, which would otherwise show floating, then puts it back on at the end. It moves at full speed. Without a potion: it crouches and keeps its armour on. A hidden bot doesn't start fights but still follows orders: it follows, retreats and holds its post. It comes out and fights as soon as an enemy is close and can see it."}, {"title": "Tether (leash)", "page": "Movement and orders", "url": "movement.html#tether", "text": "/pvpbot tether Prisoner Boss faction guards 15 /pvpbot tether info Prisoner /pvpbot tether stop Prisoner The escort follows the target and makes sure it stays within the radius (default 20) of the leader. If the target strays outside, it gets one warning : a real but weak hit (half a heart) that shoves it back toward the leader. If it comes back inside, the warning is cleared. If it isn't back within 5 seconds , the whole escort attacks it for real, as with /pvpbot attack , until you run tether stop ."}, {"title": "Pause", "page": "Movement and orders", "url": "movement.html#pause", "text": "/pvpbot pause all /pvpbot resume faction bandits A paused bot stays in the world, falls and takes damage, but does nothing. Useful to set up a scene without bots wandering off or starting a fight. /pvpbot pause with no argument lists who is paused."}, {"title": "Teleporting", "page": "Movement and orders", "url": "movement.html#teleporting", "text": "/pvpbot tp all Steve /pvpbot tp portal ground faction bandits Steve 40 /pvpbot tp pearl latest 100 70 -300 /pvpbot tp rift big overfaction north Steve Plain tp is instant. portal , pearl and rift bring the squad in with some style, scattered within a range (default 100) around the destination. Details on Commands ."}, {"title": "Scripts", "page": "Features", "url": "scripts.html", "text": "Save a sequence of commands under a name and run it whenever you like, with parameters and pauses."}, {"title": "What they're for", "page": "Scripts", "url": "scripts.html#what-they-re-for", "text": "Setting up a scenario always takes the same commands: spawn the bots, put them in a faction, hand out kits, point them at someone. A script stores those lines under a name, so one line runs them all again."}, {"title": "Example", "page": "Scripts", "url": "scripts.html#example", "text": "/pvpbot script create raid /pvpbot script add raid pvpbot spawn 20 /pvpbot script add raid pvpbot faction join {1} latest /pvpbot script add raid pvpbot kit givefaction {2} {1} /pvpbot script add raid wait 40 /pvpbot script add raid pvpbot faction hostile {1} player And to run it: /pvpbot script run raid bandits iron This spawns 20 bots, puts them in the bandits faction, gives them the iron kit, waits two seconds and then sets them at war with the players."}, {"title": "Rules", "page": "Scripts", "url": "scripts.html#rules", "text": "Parameters: {1} … {9} are replaced by the arguments given to script run , in order. Pauses: a wait <ticks> line stops the script and resumes the rest after that many ticks (20 = 1 second). The server carries on as normal meanwhile. latest: inside a script it means the bots this script created with its last spawn , even if someone else spawned bots in the meantime. Leading slash: lines are written without / ; if you add one it's removed. Comments: lines starting with # are skipped. Limit: 200 lines per script. One at a time: if you run a script while another is running, it starts as soon as the first one finishes. That way two scripts never grab each other's bots. Permissions: lines run as if you typed them. A script saves typing but grants nothing extra. Named bots in scripts /pvpbot spawn <name> looks up the skin in the background, so the next line might not find the bot yet. In scripts"}, {"title": "Managing scripts", "page": "Scripts", "url": "scripts.html#managing-scripts", "text": "Command What it does /pvpbot script list Lists the scripts. /pvpbot script show <name> Shows the numbered lines. /pvpbot script remove <name> <line> Removes a line (numbered as in show ). /pvpbot script delete <name> Deletes the script. Scripts are stored in plugins/PvpBot/scripts.yml . A /pvpbot reload stops any script that is running."}, {"title": "Companions", "page": "Features", "url": "companions.html", "text": "Hand a bot to a regular player, who orders it around by typing in chat."}, {"title": "Handing over a bot", "page": "Companions", "url": "companions.html#handing-over-a-bot", "text": "/pvpbot owner set Fido Steve /pvpbot owner list Steve /pvpbot owner clear Fido From then on Steve (even with no permissions) can order Fido around by typing in chat, the way you'd talk to a teammate. The player has to be online when you set it."}, {"title": "Chat orders", "page": "Companions", "url": "companions.html#chat-orders", "text": "A message only counts as an order if its sender owns at least one spawned bot, and the message contains the word bot (or bots ) plus one of the orders below. Orders don't show up in chat for anyone else. Everything else goes through as normal, so you can talk about bots without giving orders by accident. Type Also works Effect bot follow come, heel, escort Follows you. bot guard defend, post, hold Guards the spot you're on, within 10 blocks. bot stay wait, freeze Stays where it is (paused). bot stop halt, disengage, shutdown Stops, and won't even hit back when hit. bot free release, dismiss, roam, unleash Clears its orders: it's free again. bot attack <name> kill, fight, hunt Attacks that player or bot. bot cancel forget, calm, truce Drops whoever it's fighting, without stopping following you or guarding. bot summon teleport, warp Teleports to you and clears whatever order it was stuck o"}, {"title": "Self-defence", "page": "Companions", "url": "companions.html#self-defence", "text": "Companions always hit back at whoever hits them, even with the server's revenge setting off. The exceptions are hits from their own owner, and the bot stop order."}, {"title": "When the owner logs out", "page": "Companions", "url": "companions.html#when-the-owner-logs-out", "text": "When the owner disconnects, their bots aren't removed. They're parked high above the spawn of the companions.holding-world world and paused, with health, inventory and faction intact. They only come back when the owner types bot summon : rejoining the server isn't enough. companions: holding-world: test_world Set the right world The default is test_world . If your server has no world by that name, the bots stay where they are and the console says so: change it in config.yml to a world you have."}, {"title": "Skins and names", "page": "Features", "url": "skins.html", "text": "What bots look like and what they're called."}, {"title": "Real skins", "page": "Skins and names", "url": "skins.html#real-skins", "text": "A bot wears the skin of the Minecraft account that has its name. You can also borrow another account's skin: /pvpbot spawn Marco /pvpbot spawn Marco Notch In the first case Marco wears the \"Marco\" account's skin, if it exists. In the second he wears Notch's skin but is still called Marco. Every bot keeps its skin across restarts. If SkinsRestorer is installed, PvpBot also saves the skin in its storage, so it isn't overwritten when the bot joins."}, {"title": "Random skins", "page": "Skins and names", "url": "skins.html#random-skins", "text": "Almost every bot name is made up and has no skin, so without this option they'd all be Steve and Alex. With skins.random bots borrow a skin from a pool. Value Who gets a random skin missing (default) Bots whose name has no skin: generated names, name tables, made-up names. A bot named after a real account keeps its own. always Every bot, whatever its name. off Nobody: default skins. skins: random: missing built-in-pool: true pool: [] Built-in pool ( built-in-pool ): a few hundred ordinary players' accounts. Names still on a default skin are skipped, and a skin two accounts share is used once. Your additions ( pool ): a list of account names whose skins you want bots in. No repeats: a batch of bots goes through every skin before any repeats. First start: the skins are fetched in the background, gently, to stay under Mojang's rate limit (a minute or two; the console says how many came thro"}, {"title": "Names", "page": "Skins and names", "url": "skins.html#names", "text": ""}, {"title": "Generated names", "page": "Skins and names", "url": "skins.html#generated-names", "text": "/pvpbot spawn and /pvpbot spawn <count> generate unique random names."}, {"title": "Name tables", "page": "Skins and names", "url": "skins.html#name-tables", "text": "For a themed group (a clan's members, an event's characters) write the lists in config.yml : name-tables: pirates: - Blackbeard - Morgan - Kidd - Drake and use them like this: /pvpbot spawn names:pirates 4 /pvpbot spawn names:pirates 4 Notch Names are picked at random from the table, never twice in the same command. Names already used by a bot or an online player are skipped. If the table runs out, it spawns as many as it can and tells you. With an account name at the end, the whole batch wears that account's skin. Names can be 16 characters at most."}, {"title": "No join messages", "page": "Skins and names", "url": "skins.html#no-join-messages", "text": "Bots appear without a \"X joined the game\" message, so a squad of twenty doesn't flood the chat."}, {"title": "Ghosts", "page": "Features", "url": "ghosts.html", "text": "Scenic NPCs that replay a recorded path or stare at you from a distance. They don't fight and can't be touched."}, {"title": "What they are", "page": "Ghosts", "url": "ghosts.html#what-they-are", "text": "A ghost is a fake player like the bots, but kept out of everything else in the plugin. It doesn't fight, doesn't move on its own, doesn't count as a bot and doesn't show up in /pvpbot list . It can't be damaged, hit or interacted with. It appears and disappears with no chat message. Use ghosts for atmosphere, horror events and scenes. The /ghost commands need pvpbot.admin ."}, {"title": "Recording a path", "page": "Ghosts", "url": "ghosts.html#recording-a-path", "text": "/ghost record lap /ghost stoprecord Between the two commands PvpBot records your position every tick. It also records the blocks you break or place, and puts them back afterwards so the world stays clean. /ghost recordings lists the recordings, which are saved in plugins/PvpBot/ghost-recordings/ ."}, {"title": "A ghost that replays the path", "page": "Ghosts", "url": "ghosts.html#a-ghost-that-replays-the-path", "text": "/ghost spawn Wanderer lap The ghost replays the path forever, exactly where you recorded it. It also redoes the block changes at the same moments and undoes them before starting over, so every lap starts from the same world."}, {"title": "A ghost that stares", "page": "Ghosts", "url": "ghosts.html#a-ghost-that-stares", "text": "/ghost stare Watcher The ghost stands still and turns to face the nearest player. When a player gets within 6 blocks it silently vanishes. For the first 5 seconds after it's created it won't vanish, so it doesn't disappear in front of whoever just spawned it."}, {"title": "Skins and lines", "page": "Ghosts", "url": "ghosts.html#skins-and-lines", "text": "Both kinds accept a skin and some lines at the end of the command: With no skin: the ghost wears the skin of the account with its name; skin:<name> borrows another account's skin; skin:none uses the default one. Lines: separated by | . The ghost says one in chat every 6 seconds, in turn, under its own name. /ghost stare Steve skin:none Who's there?|...|Leave now /ghost spawn Keeper lap skin:Notch You shouldn't be here"}, {"title": "Managing ghosts", "page": "Ghosts", "url": "ghosts.html#managing-ghosts", "text": "/ghost list /ghost remove Watcher"}, {"title": "Troubleshooting", "page": "Help", "url": "troubleshooting.html", "text": "The usual reasons a bot won't move, won't take damage or won't attack, and how to fix them. Always start here /pvpbot diagnose <bot> checks the bot and tells you what's blocking it: game mode, damage immunity, login plugins, plugins cancelling damage, movement or knockback, and the last knockback it took. In the next update , /pvpbot diagnose <player> does the same from the other side: why bots can't hurt a particular player."}, {"title": "The bot hops on the spot and won't move", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "troubleshooting.html#the-bot-hops-on-the-spot-and-won-t-move", "text": "It's almost always a login plugin (AuthMe, LoginTo…). A bot can't type /login , so the plugin keeps it locked. Check that auth-autologin is true (it is by default): it registers bots as already logged in. If your login plugin isn't one PvpBot recognises, /pvpbot diagnose shows it. In that case set the login plugin to ignore players with the bots' names, or disable it to test."}, {"title": "The bot takes no damage", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "troubleshooting.html#the-bot-takes-no-damage", "text": "Invulnerable flag: some login plugins set it and never clear it. With force-vulnerable: true (the default) PvpBot clears it. diagnose tells you if this is the cause. Region protection or PvP off: WorldGuard, safe zones and the like cancel damage. It's better to move the fight out of the protected area. As a last resort there's /pvpbot set bypass-damage-protection true , but it overrides all protection, legitimate protection included. Game mode: a bot in creative or spectator takes no damage."}, {"title": "The bot won't attack me", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "troubleshooting.html#the-bot-won-t-attack-me", "text": "It was just spawned: it waits spawn-grace seconds, then sizes you up for reaction-ticks . It can't see you: with detection on it needs line of sight. Crouching or invisible, you're noticed from closer. You're in creative or spectator ( attack-invincible is off). A preset made it passive ( /pvpbot preset shows which is active), or auto-target or target-players are off, perhaps just for its faction ( /pvpbot faction info ). You're allies or at peace: see Factions . It's paused, hiding or retreating."}, {"title": "The bot fights with its fists", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "troubleshooting.html#the-bot-fights-with-its-fists", "text": "It has no weapons. Give it a kit or /pvpbot givebow <bot> . auto-equip must be on for it to hold the best weapon by itself."}, {"title": "Lag with lots of bots", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "troubleshooting.html#lag-with-lots-of-bots", "text": "Raise target-scan-interval (say from 10 to 20): looking for targets is the most expensive thing a bot does. When its target dies or leaves, a bot still looks again at once. Lower max-target-distance . Turn wander off for bots that don't need to move, or pause them."}, {"title": "Odd stats or speed after a boss or an item", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "troubleshooting.html#odd-stats-or-speed-after-a-boss-or-an-item", "text": "Another plugin added an attribute modifier (health, speed, damage) and never removed it. /pvpbot resetattr <player|bot> removes every modifier and restores a player's correct base values. It works on real players too."}, {"title": "The world folder fills up with bot files", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "troubleshooting.html#the-world-folder-fills-up-with-bot-files", "text": "Bots are real players, so the server saves data, stats and advancements for them like anyone else. /pvpbot clearplayerdata deletes those files for bots that no longer exist. It leaves active bots, kits, factions, scripts and config alone."}, {"title": "The plugin loads but nothing works", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "troubleshooting.html#the-plugin-loads-but-nothing-works", "text": "Check you installed the right jar for your platform and version (see Installation ). Read the console at startup. A kits.yml copied from another server may fail to load: PvpBot still starts, without kits, and says so in the console."}, {"title": "Companions aren't put away", "page": "Troubleshooting", "url": "troubleshooting.html#companions-aren-t-put-away", "text": "Check companions.holding-world : it must be the name of a world that exists on your server. See Companions ."}, {"title": "Spigot edition", "page": "Help", "url": "spigot.html", "text": "How the Paper and Spigot jars differ, and how to build the Spigot edition."}, {"title": "One plugin, two jars", "page": "Spigot edition", "url": "spigot.html#one-plugin-two-jars", "text": "The Spigot edition (1.21.11 and 26.2) has the same AI, commands, config and features as the Paper edition. It's built from the same source, with a few files swapped for Spigot versions. It's a separate jar so the Paper build loses nothing: Paper keeps its own faster calls, and neither jar carries code for the other server."}, {"title": "What differs inside", "page": "Spigot edition", "url": "spigot.html#what-differs-inside", "text": "Part Paper Spigot Chat Adventure components Classic colour codes, rendered to look the same Events AsyncChatEvent , Paper's knockback event AsyncPlayerChatEvent , Bukkit's knockback event Platform Paper methods (current tick, replaceable blocks, nearby players…) The equivalent vanilla or Bukkit calls Server access Direct CraftBukkit casts CraftBukkit found at startup (Spigot puts the revision in the package name) Bot skins Paper's profile API Swaps the profile directly Finished loading Fields looked up by name The \"player loaded\" packet a real client sends For anyone using the plugin nothing changes: the commands and config.yml are identical."}, {"title": "Building the Spigot edition", "page": "Spigot edition", "url": "spigot.html#building-the-spigot-edition", "text": "You need Spigot's BuildTools , run with --remapped for each version: java -jar BuildTools.jar --rev 1.21.11 --remapped java -jar BuildTools.jar --rev 26.2 --remapped Copy the spigot-<version>.jar files BuildTools produces into .spigot/ at the repo root. Run pvpbot-spigot/build.sh 1.21.11 26.2 . The jars end up in pvpbot-spigot/dist-<version>/PvpBot-<version>.jar . If BuildTools rejects --remapped for a version, build that version without it: the script then compiles against the server jar directly. Variable Default Meaning SPIGOT_JARS .spigot/ Where the Spigot jars are. M2 ~/.m2/repository The local Maven repository (where --remapped installs the mappings). JDK java on the PATH At least 21 for 1.21.11, at least 25 for 26.2. The script works on Linux, macOS and Git Bash on Windows."}, {"title": "Status", "page": "Spigot edition", "url": "spigot.html#status", "text": "Every change to the Spigot edition is checked against the Bukkit API, and the check fails if it uses anything only Paper has. It hasn't been run on a live Spigot server yet: if you hit a problem, please report it."}];
